package com.hpcai270

// Days 169–185: performance engineering, benchmarking and bottleneck analysis.
val hpcDaysPart16 = listOf(
Day(169,"Performance mental model","Latency, bandwidth, IOPS and concurrency","Performance is multi-dimensional. Latency is time per operation; bandwidth is data per second; IOPS is operations per second; concurrency determines how much work is outstanding.","MODEL\n\nThroughput depends on operation size × operations/sec. Latency and throughput are related but not interchangeable.","AI\n\nTraining may need sustained bandwidth while metadata operations need low latency/high IOPS. Checkpointing may create large synchronized writes.","LAB\n\nCreate four workload examples and label the dominant metric. Explain why optimizing the wrong metric produces little benefit.","INTERVIEW\n\nCan a system have high IOPS but low throughput? Yes: small I/O. Can it have high throughput but poor latency? Yes: large sequential operations can hide latency behind concurrency.","GOAL\n\nChoose the metric before choosing the optimization."),
Day(170,"Little's Law","Connecting concurrency, throughput and latency","Little's Law provides a powerful queueing intuition: average number of items in a stable system equals throughput multiplied by average time in the system.","FORM\n\nL = λW\n\nIf throughput rises while latency stays constant, outstanding work rises. If concurrency is fixed, throughput eventually becomes constrained by latency.","STORAGE\n\nQueue depth is outstanding I/O. The relationship helps explain why increasing queue depth can raise throughput until saturation, then mainly increase latency.","LAB\n\nUse hypothetical values to calculate outstanding I/O from IOPS and latency. Then explain why the calculation is an approximation when workload conditions are unstable.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy does blindly increasing queue depth often make a slow system worse? Because it can increase contention and tail latency after the useful parallelism is exhausted.","GOAL\n\nUse queueing theory to reason about performance instead of guessing."),
Day(171,"Amdahl's law","The bottleneck limits the speedup","Amdahl's law explains why optimizing one component cannot produce unlimited end-to-end speedup when another fraction of the workload remains serial or slow.","EXAMPLE\n\nIf 80% of a workload can be accelerated 10×, the remaining 20% still limits total speedup. The exact result depends on the workload fractions.","AI\n\nMaking storage 2× faster does not make training 2× faster if preprocessing, GPU compute or synchronization dominates.","LAB\n\nCreate a hypothetical pipeline with CPU, storage, network and GPU fractions. Calculate which optimization has the largest theoretical impact.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should storage architects care about application profiling? Because the fastest storage is useless if it is not on the critical path.","GOAL\n\nOptimize the critical path, not the most impressive component."),
Day(172,"Roofline thinking","Compute versus memory bandwidth","The Roofline model helps reason about whether a workload is compute-bound or memory-bandwidth-bound. In AI systems the same idea extends to storage and network feeding the compute.","MODEL\n\nOperational intensity relates computation to data movement. Low arithmetic intensity can make bandwidth critical; high intensity can make compute dominant.","AI\n\nA GPU can be extremely fast while the input pipeline cannot feed it. The infrastructure problem is then data movement rather than GPU capacity.","LAB\n\nClassify three workloads: matrix multiply, dataset decoding and checkpoint write. Identify likely bottlenecks.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you prove a GPU is starved by storage? Correlate GPU utilization, input wait, storage throughput/latency and network telemetry under controlled tests.","GOAL\n\nThink in data movement as well as FLOPS."),
Day(173,"FIO mastery I","Workload construction","FIO is a flexible I/O workload generator. Senior engineers should understand every important parameter rather than copy a benchmark command.","PARAMETERS\n\nfilename/device, ioengine, rw, bs, iodepth, numjobs, runtime, direct, size, time_based, group_reporting.","DESIGN\n\nRandom read IOPS, random write IOPS, sequential read bandwidth and sequential write bandwidth are distinct tests.","SAFETY\n\nNever run destructive FIO against a production block device. Use a dedicated test file/device and verify the target before writing.","LAB\n\nCreate four FIO profiles and predict the result before running. Compare prediction to measurement.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain `iodepth` versus `numjobs`. Both can increase concurrency but operate at different levels of the workload model.","GOAL\n\nRead and design FIO tests confidently."),
Day(174,"FIO mastery II","Interpreting latency and percentiles","A benchmark result is more than bandwidth. Examine IOPS, average latency, completion latency percentiles, CPU utilization and queue depth.","LATENCY\n\nAverages can hide tail spikes. p99/p99.9 can reveal stalls that affect synchronized applications.","CPU\n\nHigh I/O throughput with excessive CPU may indicate an inefficient I/O path. Low CPU with poor throughput can indicate backend or network constraints.","LAB\n\nCompare two hypothetical systems with identical average latency but different p99. Decide which is better for a synchronized workload.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat FIO evidence would make you suspect queue saturation? Rising latency with little throughput gain as depth increases.","GOAL\n\nInterpret benchmark curves, not isolated numbers."),
Day(175,"IOR mastery","Parallel filesystem benchmark design","IOR is widely used for parallel filesystem testing. Its value comes from representing multi-client parallel I/O rather than a single local disk.","VARIABLES\n\nclients/processes, transfer size, block size, shared file vs file-per-process, read/write mode and collective behavior.","SCALING\n\nMeasure 1, 2, 4, 8, 16… clients. Plot aggregate bandwidth and per-client fairness. Look for the knee where adding clients stops helping.","AI\n\nThe knee can identify storage/network saturation and guide how many concurrent workers the training pipeline should use.","LAB\n\nDesign an IOR scaling experiment and specify warm-up, repetitions and reporting format.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can aggregate bandwidth rise while per-client bandwidth falls? Shared resources become saturated.","GOAL\n\nMeasure scale, not just peak."),
Day(176,"Storage bottleneck taxonomy","Find the first saturated resource","A bottleneck can be media, controller CPU, storage network, host CPU, filesystem metadata, queueing, application synchronization or cache behavior.","FAULT TREE\n\nLow throughput → check workload → client CPU → queue depth → network → server CPU → backend media → filesystem state → application behavior.","RULE\n\nFind the first resource whose saturation explains the observed symptom. Do not stop at the first abnormal metric.","LAB\n\nCreate five scenarios and identify the likely first bottleneck from a set of counters.","INTERVIEW\n\nStorage latency is high. What evidence distinguishes backend media saturation from network congestion? Compare device latency, NIC counters, RTT, server CPU and client observations.","GOAL\n\nTrace causality across layers."),
Day(177,"Cache effects","When the benchmark is measuring memory","Caches can make storage appear much faster than the physical media. Understand page cache, application cache, storage controller cache and filesystem cache behavior.","QUESTION\n\nWhat exactly is being measured? If data is already in RAM, the test may not exercise storage media.","CONTROL\n\nUse appropriate direct I/O or cache-warm/cold methodology when valid. Document cache state rather than hiding it.","AI\n\nRepeated dataset reads may legitimately benefit from caching. That is a feature when it represents production behavior, not a benchmark error.","LAB\n\nDesign warm-cache and cold-cache tests and state what each tells you.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can two identical benchmarks produce different results after reboot? Cache state and background recovery/workload can differ.","GOAL\n\nKnow what your benchmark is actually measuring."),
Day(178,"CPU and storage overlap","Asynchronous I/O and pipeline efficiency","High-performance systems overlap computation and I/O. A well-designed application can process one batch while another is being loaded or written.","PIPELINE\n\nRead → preprocess → transfer → compute → checkpoint/write, with stages overlapped where possible.","BOTTLENECK\n\nIf stages are serialized, faster storage may simply shorten one wait while another stage dominates.","AI\n\nDataLoader workers, prefetching, pinned memory and asynchronous copies are application-side mechanisms that interact with storage infrastructure.","LAB\n\nDraw a serial versus pipelined training input path. Identify where storage latency can be hidden.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy might increasing storage bandwidth have almost no effect on training time? The application may not overlap I/O or another stage dominates.","GOAL\n\nOptimize the pipeline, not only the storage box."),
Day(179,"GPU utilization analysis","Use GPU metrics to find infrastructure problems","GPU utilization is a symptom, not a root cause. Low utilization can come from input stalls, CPU preprocessing, synchronization, memory limits or model behavior.","EVIDENCE\n\nGPU utilization, SM occupancy, memory utilization, PCIe traffic, NVLink traffic, CPU usage, storage throughput, network traffic and application timing.","PATTERN\n\nPeriodic GPU idle gaps aligned with data reads suggest input pipeline stalls. Long synchronized gaps across GPUs can indicate collective or storage synchronization.","LAB\n\nConstruct three timelines: storage-limited, CPU-limited and network-limited. State the metric that separates them.","INTERVIEW\n\n‘GPU is only 50% utilized; buy faster storage.’ Why is that premature? Explain profiling and correlation.","GOAL\n\nUse GPU telemetry as part of infrastructure troubleshooting."),
Day(180,"Checkpoint performance","The write storm problem","Checkpoints are large, often synchronized writes. If many workers write at once, storage and network load can spike dramatically.","PATTERN\n\nTraining steps → synchronization → checkpoint creation → many concurrent writes → metadata + data burst → return to training.","DESIGN\n\nCheckpoint interval, shard size, parallel write strategy, filesystem striping, network capacity, caching and background persistence all matter.","FAILURE\n\nIf checkpoint storage is slow, GPUs may idle while waiting. If checkpoint writes are too aggressive, they can interfere with active reads.","LAB\n\nDesign checkpoint storage for 256 GPUs producing 4 TB checkpoints every 30 minutes. State bandwidth target and headroom assumptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you reduce checkpoint impact without losing recovery capability? Discuss asynchronous staging, local NVMe, parallel writes and durable persistence.","GOAL\n\nTreat checkpointing as a workload, not a file-copy event."),
Day(181,"Small-file explosion","The hidden AI metadata problem","AI data pipelines often create many shards, manifests, token files, logs and checkpoints. Millions of small files can consume metadata resources and slow job startup.","SYMPTOMS\n\nSlow dataset discovery, slow directory operations, high metadata CPU, low bulk bandwidth and many simultaneous opens/stats.","MITIGATIONS\n\nBundle files into larger containers where application semantics allow, use optimized dataset formats, cache locally, tune metadata services and avoid shared hot directories.","LAB\n\nDesign a dataset with 10 million 1 MB files and an equivalent packed representation. Compare metadata operation counts conceptually.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can packing files improve training startup even if total bytes remain unchanged? Metadata operations fall dramatically.","GOAL\n\nRecognize file-count as a resource."),
Day(182,"Storage scaling curves","Scale-up, scale-out and the knee","Scale-up increases resources in a system; scale-out adds nodes/targets. Neither automatically produces linear performance.","CURVE\n\n1 client → 2 → 4 → 8 → 16 → 32. Plot throughput. Identify linear region, saturation knee and possible degradation.","CAUSES\n\nShared links, metadata services, CPU, synchronization, controller limits, network topology and backend media can all flatten scaling.","LAB\n\nCreate a hypothetical curve and identify where adding hardware stops producing proportional benefit.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat does ‘linear scaling’ mean? It means useful performance increases approximately in proportion to resources under the defined workload and conditions.","GOAL\n\nUse scaling evidence to justify architecture."),
Day(183,"Fairness and noisy neighbors","Performance in shared clusters","A cluster can have high aggregate throughput and still deliver poor user experience if one workload dominates resources.","RESOURCES\n\nBandwidth, IOPS, metadata, CPU, network queues and storage targets can all be shared.","CONTROLS\n\nScheduler limits, QoS, project quotas, separate tiers, network classes and workload placement can reduce interference.","AI\n\nOne massive training run can consume the storage fabric while smaller interactive jobs wait. Operational policy is part of performance.","LAB\n\nDesign QoS for three classes: interactive research, training and background archive movement.","INTERVIEW\n\nWould you maximize aggregate storage throughput or fairness? Answer: define the service objective first.","GOAL\n\nTreat multi-tenancy as a performance design problem."),
Day(184,"Performance incident simulation","From symptom to root cause","Practice a realistic incident: training throughput drops 35%, storage latency rises, network utilization is 60%, and only one workload is affected.","HYPOTHESES\n\nBackend saturation, target imbalance, metadata issue, client queueing, network congestion, application behavior or workload change.","METHOD\n\nCompare before/after, scope affected clients, inspect per-target/per-client metrics, correlate timestamps and test a reversible hypothesis.","DECISION\n\nIf only one target is hot, suspect placement/striping. If all targets are hot, suspect workload or aggregate capacity. If network queues spike, investigate fabric.","LAB\n\nWrite a complete incident report with evidence and rejected hypotheses.","INTERVIEW\n\nThe strongest answer explains why each hypothesis was accepted or rejected.","GOAL\n\nBuild senior troubleshooting muscle memory."),
Day(185,"Performance engineering checkpoint","Defend a benchmark","You should now be able to create FIO/IOR tests, interpret latency distributions, explain queue depth, scaling, cache, GPU starvation and checkpoint behavior.","EXAM\n\nDesign a benchmark for a 512-GPU storage platform. Define workload, client count, concurrency, success criteria and observability.","ANALYSIS\n\nExplain what would make you increase queue depth, add storage nodes, add network capacity or change filesystem layout.","CAPSTONE\n\nProduce a graph of throughput versus client count and identify the scaling knee. Explain the physical resource at the knee.","INTERVIEW\n\n‘How do you validate a vendor’s 1 TB/s claim?’ Answer with workload definition, controlled testing, application validation and tail behavior.","STANDARD\n\nYou should be able to challenge performance claims professionally using measurements.")
)
